118.83621215820312,"height":34.581336975097656}},{"text":"Variance– Reflects the model's responsiveness to small fluctuations or noise in training data. A model with high variance excels on its training data by accounting for random noise, but falters on new, unseen data (overfitting). ","targetRectangle":{"y":599.03035545349121,"x":136.60696887969971,"width":578.20000000000005,"height":160.27482604980469}},{"text":"With high variance, adding more data can often boost performance since it helps \\\"average out\\\" or reduce the noise captured by the model.","targetRectangle":{"y":760.03035545349121,"x":136.60696887969971,"width":584.57500000000016,"height":99.274826049804688}},{"text":"￼","targetRectangle":{"y":1049.9674994945526,"x":11.404099398001563,"width":20.015999999999998,"height":21.496002197265625}},{"text":"BIAS & Variance [Flashback to l#1]","targetRectangle":{"y":56.913127899169922,"x":66.77398681640625,"width":1152.6800000000003,"height":79.599995136260986}},{"text":"pasted-image.tiff","targetRectangle":{"y":1025.85791015625,"x":1546.1016387939453,"width":222.40380859375,"height":40.216812133789062}},{"text":"© Zahra Shakeri, Dalla Lana School of Public Health, University of Toronto","targetRectangle":{"y":1055.1609382629395,"x":54.743599891662598,"width":495.66000000000008,"height":16.580001831054688}},{"text":"Bias– Bias refers to the error from simplifying a real-world problem with an overly simple model. High bias can cause the model to overlook important relationships between input features and output predictions. ","targetRectangle":{"y":234.16407269239426,"x":143.01136779785156,"width":603.97500000000002,"height":131.27482604980469}},{"text":"With high bias, adding more data might not help as the model is too basic to grasp data complexities.","targetRectangle":{"y":366.16407269239426,"x":143.01136779785156,"width":597,"height":69.274826049804688}}],"baseLayer":{"animations":[],"initialState":{"affineTransform":[1,0,0,1,0,0],"masksToBounds":false